title: "BORDER OPTICS: Satellite Verification of India's Vibrant Villages Programme"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
    description: "Zenodo archive of BORDER OPTICS v1.0.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
abstract: >-
  An independent satellite-verification study of India's Vibrant Villages
  Programme (VVP-I), a ₹4,800 crore border-area development scheme sanctioned
  across 2,967 villages in five Himalayan border states/union territories.
  Using Sentinel-2 built-up-index (NDBI) and VIIRS night-lights data at 258
  individually geocoded villages against a district-restricted non-VVP
  control group of 721 villages in the same 14 districts (district
  membership independently verified against real administrative boundaries
  for all but one district), this study tests
  whether VVP-I's priority villages show measurable physical development
  since programme sanction, under two independent compositing windows
  (full-year and summer-matched) as an explicit robustness check rather than
  trusting a single satellite comparison. Two earlier versions of this study
  each found a significant result that turned out, on inspection, to be a
  data artifact rather than a real effect (an incomplete satellite extraction,
  then a contaminated control group); once both were fixed, the primary
  NDBI/night-lights control-group DiD result was a clean null in both
  windows, at all three tested buffer radii (250m/500m/1km), under
  leave-one-district-out and randomization-inference checks. This version
  subjects that null to two further tests it had not yet faced: an exact
  wild cluster bootstrap (16,384 sign-flip combinations) addressing the
  design's reliance on only 14 district clusters, and a genuine
  parallel-pre-trends placebo test using a second pre-treatment year (2019)
  for both treated and control villages. Both confirm the null (wild-cluster
  p = 0.32-0.50 across all four outcome/window combinations; 3 of 4 placebo
  comparisons clean, the fourth borderline and reported as such). A
  border-proximity correlation flagged in the previous release as new and
  unchecked has since been run through the same leave-one-district-out,
  randomization-inference, and a further spatial-autocorrelation-calibrated
  permutation test, and survives all three (spatially-corrected p = 0.0035
  full-year night-lights, p = 0.0295 summer NDBI) — a real secondary finding,
  running opposite to the direction predicted by securitization theory. Most
  significantly, triangulating the primary NDBI/night-lights design against
  two secondary satellite proxies of different kinds — Sentinel-1 SAR
  backscatter (a genuinely different sensor, radar not optical) and Dynamic
  World's machine-learned built-up probability (a different algorithm, but
  still derived from the same Sentinel-2 imagery as NDBI, so
  algorithm-independent rather than sensor-independent) — did not simply
  confirm the null: SAR agrees (null in both windows), but Dynamic World's
  control-group DiD is significant in both windows (p = 0.0117 full-year,
  p = 0.0013 summer), and Dynamic World validates far more strongly
  against real, non-satellite ground truth (Google Open Buildings footprint
  counts, ρ = 0.808 vs. NDBI's ρ = 0.399) than the primary measure does. At
  three villages with independently confirmed real construction, Dynamic
  World and night-lights both correctly detected the increase while NDBI
  moved in the wrong direction at all three. This tension between proxies is
  reported openly as an unresolved question — whether NDBI under-detects a
  real, modest effect or Dynamic World's result is itself a different kind
  of false positive — rather than smoothed into either a confirmed null or a
  confirmed effect. A second thing initially looked like a second, unrelated
  sensitivity: cross-checking the QA60 cloud mask used throughout against an
  alternative SCL-band mask, holding the village sample fixed at the 200
  villages both masks could then produce a valid summer composite for,
  flipped the primary NDBI result from null (QA60, p = 0.449) to significant
  and positive (SCL, p = 0.0000045). It turned out to be a bug, caught by
  external review rather than this project's own checks: the SCL mask's
  "clear" class list was letting snow pixels through as clear ground, a real
  risk for June-September composites over Himalayan villages. Fixed and
  rerun against live Earth Engine, SCL coverage rose to 251 of 258 villages
  (matching QA60's own coverage) and the result is no longer significant
  (mean change +0.00112, p = 0.288), agreeing with QA60's own null on the
  same 251 villages (p = 0.436) — reported as a bug this project's external
  review caught and this project then fixed and re-verified, not as a second
  unresolved measurement tension. One further correction: a district-
  verification rerun closed the control list's remaining data-quality gap
  for two of its three affected districts, taking the list from 732 to 721
  villages; every number in this study reflects that corrected list, and
  none change conclusion, except that the full-year night-lights baseline
  balance check is newly imbalanced (p = 0.036) where it previously was not
  (p = 0.090) — disclosed as a genuine finding rather than smoothed over.
  This study addresses a parliamentary
  record showing no independent impact assessment has ever been carried out
  for VVP-I.
keywords:
